From 601ee683a54827544cb1ea34bca5ec7f9ad48a54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:49:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: say why BD-J menus can or cannot run in the BD-J
 service

The Java code of a Blu-ray disc no longer runs in Kodi. libbluray hands a disc with BD-J titles
over to the BD-J service of the system (hades-bdj): a process of its own for each disc, with the
Java runtime that comes with the system (/usr/lib/hades-bdj/jre), which sees the disc only
through Kodi and ends with it. Nothing has to be installed, so what BlurayJava told the user
(install "JRE for BD-J menus", restart Kodi, correct JAVA_HOME, a Java that is too new) no
longer applies.

libbluray says whether it can hand discs over (BLURAY_DISC_INFO::bdj_handled); where it cannot,
the files and the socket of the service say why:
- NO_SERVICE: the program of the service (/usr/lib/hades-bdj/hades-bdj-host) or its Java runtime
  (the "release" file of /usr/lib/hades-bdj/jre) is missing;
- NO_JAR: the Java part of this libbluray version (libbluray-j2se-<version>.jar and
  libbluray-awt-j2se-<version>.jar in /usr/share/java) is missing;
- SERVICE_NOT_RUNNING: the service is installed and its socket (/run/hades-bdj/bdj.sock, or what
  LIBBLURAY_BDJ_HOST_SOCKET names, as libbluray reads it) is not there;
- UNAVAILABLE: all of it is there, and libbluray says that it cannot hand discs to it; its own
  lines in kodi.log say why;
- START_FAILED: as before, libbluray reported BD_ERROR_BDJ on a disc that it could hand over.
The order is that of what a user or a log reader can act on: the service first, then the jar,
then the socket. What libbluray says always wins: READY needs nothing else.

kodi.log says, as before, which one it is, with the paths; for READY also the Java version of
the runtime (JAVA_VERSION of its "release" file) and whether BD-Live, the Internet for the Java
of a disc, is on (bluray.hadesbdlive). The service has no network yet (its unit has
PrivateNetwork=: the names of the Internet are resolved by a server of the local network, which
it must not reach), so with BD-Live on the line says that the Java of a disc does not reach the
Internet; SERVICE_HAS_NETWORK is the one place to change with the unit.

Where the add-on "JRE for BD-J menus" (tools.jre.zulu) is installed, kodi.log says once in each
run of Kodi that it is not used and can be uninstalled; the user is not told anything about it.

ReportBdj() takes only bdj_handled: libjvm_detected is about a JVM in the process, which the
client side of libbluray never loads. JavaFeatureVersion() and the rule of Java 24 and later go:
Kodi's process never starts a JVM, and the runtime of the service is the one of the system.

Texts: the block of BD-J texts keeps its numbers, with new texts for the new states (#39866
NO_SERVICE, #39867 SERVICE_NOT_RUNNING, #39868 UNAVAILABLE, #39869 NO_JAR, #39885 START_FAILED;
in Estuary #32119 to #32122 and #32288, in Spanish as es_es, es_ar and es_mx). #39884 and
